# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# CREDENTIALS -- these must never enter the repository.
# If one ever does, rotate it first and scrub history second: a secret that has
# been committed is compromised even after the commit is removed.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
kaggle.json
.kaggle/
**/kaggle.json
access_token
*.pem
*.key
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml
.hf_token
huggingface_token*

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Generated artefacts
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Training output. The 12 trained checkpoints are the exception: 5.7 MB total,
# and the deployed Streamlit demo needs them. Without them every learned
# scheduler in the dropdown silently substitutes an analytic policy -- the demo
# stays honest, because the agent renames itself, but a judge clicking
# "Occupancy predictor" would be shown UCB1.
runs/*
!runs/checkpoints/
runs/checkpoints/*
!runs/checkpoints/*_history.json
!runs/checkpoints/*_trainlog.json
# Never commit a partial run: this sidecar marks a checkpoint as mid-training.
runs/checkpoints/*_progress.json
runs/checkpoints/quarantine/

# A staging drop from a remote training job, holding the candidate checkpoints
# that `runs/checkpoints` is chosen FROM: several tiers and dates side by side,
# named by run rather than by tier. Only the winners get installed into
# `runs/checkpoints`, so this directory is scratch. The weights here are already
# covered by the `*.pt` and `*.onnx` rules below, but the loose `hist_*.json`
# histories matched nothing and would otherwise be committed as untracked
# strays that shadow the real `runs/checkpoints/*_history.json`.
/out/
# Benchmark output. The *published* results are the exception, for the same
# reason the checkpoints are: a clone with no results in it cannot be checked.
# Every number quoted in README.md and docs/architecture.md comes from these
# files, and a reader who cannot open them has to take the claims on trust --
# which is the one thing this project keeps saying it will not ask for.
# Regenerable, but only by re-running a 39-minute 13-agent x 3-tier x 30-seed
# grid, so shipping them is what makes the numbers checkable in practice.
reports/*
!reports/leaderboard.md
!reports/leaderboard.tex
!reports/metrics_*.json
# The analysis side-studies the docs cite by name.
!reports/ablation.json
!reports/coverage_weight_hazard.json
!reports/coverage_weight_sweep.json
!reports/diversity_sweep.json
!reports/external_tsrd.json
!reports/external_tsrd_scan.json
!reports/latency_budget.json
!reports/predictor_replication.json
!reports/robustness.json
!reports/scan_on_scan.json
# The problem statement's figures of merit, in one auditable table. Both
# halves ship: the markdown is what a reader opens, the json is what a
# script re-checks.
!reports/figures_of_merit.json
!reports/figures_of_merit.md
!reports/hard_tier_ceiling.json
!reports/hard_tier_ceiling.md
!reports/hard_retrain_eval.json
# Figures F1-F7, referenced from the README and the architecture note.
!reports/*.png
/build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
wandb/
lightning_logs/

# Model weights and datasets are large and reproducible from seeds; they are
# distributed as Kaggle artefacts rather than in git.
*.pt
# ...except the shipped checkpoints. This exception sits AFTER the blanket rule
# because gitignore resolves by LAST match, not by proximity to the runs/ block.
!runs/checkpoints/*.pt
*.pth
*.onnx
*.npz
*.parquet
# ...except the tidy grid output. Same trap as the checkpoints above: this has
# to sit AFTER the blanket rule, because gitignore resolves by LAST match.
!reports/results.parquet
!tests/**/*.npz

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Python
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
.venv/
venv/
# Anchored to the repo root. Unanchored, `env/` also matches `smartscan/env/`
# and silently drops the entire RF environment simulator from the repository --
# which it did: the published package imported but had no `smartscan.env`.
/env/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Notebooks and editors
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Scratch work
scripts/_*.py
/tmp/

# Build logs
*.log
